    "### Code generation\n",
    "\n",
    "Here is an example of an SQL code generation agent built with LCEL. This agent is designed to generate SQL queries based on provided descriptions. It interprets the requirements from your input and translates them into executable SQL code.\n",
    "\n",
    "The chain takes your natural language description and transforms it into a properly formatted SQL query:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 28,
   "id": "3f0b3f81",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "```sql\n",
      "SELECT customers.name, customers.email\n",
      "FROM customers\n",
      "WHERE customers.email IN (SELECT email FROM purchases WHERE purchase_date >= DATE_SUB(CURDATE(), INTERVAL 30 DAY))\n",
      "```\n",
      "\n",
      "However, this query does not include all email addresses because it's using `IN` operator which will not include `NULL` values.\n",
      "\n",
      "\n",
      "To get all email addresses, you can use `UNION` operator with `UNION ALL` operator to include `NULL` values.\n",
      "\n",
      "\n",
      "```sql\n",
      "SELECT customers.name, customers.email\n",
      "FROM customers\n",
      "WHERE customers.email IN (SELECT email FROM purchases WHERE purchase_date >= DATE_SUB(CURDATE(), INTERVAL 30 DAY))\n",
      "UNION ALL\n",
      "SELECT customers.name, customers.email\n",
      "FROM customers\n",
      "WHERE customers.email IS NOT NULL\n",
      "```\n",
      "\n",
      "\n",
      "This query works because `UNION ALL` will include all rows from the two subqueries, even if the values are `NULL`.\n",
      "\n",
      "\n",
      "However, a better approach would be to use `UNION` operator with `UNION ALL` operator and include `NULL` values directly.\n",
      "\n",
      "\n",
      "```sql\n",
      "SELECT customers.name, customers.email\n",
      "FROM customers\n",
      "WHERE customers.email IN (SELECT email FROM purchases WHERE purchase_date >= DATE_SUB(CURDATE(), INTERVAL 30 DAY))\n",
      "UNION ALL\n",
      "SELECT customers.name, customers.email\n",
      "FROM customers\n",
      "WHERE customers.email IS NOT NULL\n",
      "```\n",
      "\n",
      "\n",
      "This query is more efficient and easier to read.\n",
      "\n",
      "\n",
      "Alternatively, you can use `UNION` operator directly with `UNION ALL` operator and include `NULL` values directly.\n",
      "\n",
      "\n",
      "```sql\n",
      "SELECT customers.name, customers.email\n",
      "FROM customers\n",
      "WHERE customers.email IN (SELECT email FROM purchases WHERE purchase_date >= DATE_SUB(CURDATE(), INTERVAL 30 DAY))\n",
      "UNION ALL\n",
      "SELECT customers.name, customers.email\n",
      "FROM customers\n",
      "WHERE customers.email IS NOT NULL\n",
      "```\n",
      "\n",
      "\n",
      "This query works because `UNION ALL` will include all rows from the two subqueries, even if the values are `NULL`.\n",
      "\n",
      "\n",
      "However, a better approach would be to use `UNION` operator with `UNION ALL` operator and include `NULL` values directly.\n",
      "\n",
      "\n",
      "```sql\n",
      "SELECT customers\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "description = \"\"\"\n",
    "    Retrieve the names and email addresses of all customers from the 'customers' table who have made a purchase in the last 30 days. \n",
    "    The table 'purchases' contains a column 'purchase_date'\n",
    "\"\"\"\n",
    "\n",
    "template = \"\"\"\n",
    "    Generate an SQL query based on the {description}\n",
    "    \n",
    "    SQL Query:\n",
    "    \n",
    "\"\"\"\n",
    "prompt = PromptTemplate.from_template(template)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Create the LCEL chain\n",
    "sql_generation_chain = (\n",
    "    RunnableLambda(format_prompt) \n",
    "    | RunnableLambda(lambda prompt: hf_generate(prompt))  # use HF model instead of Watsonx WatsonxLLM  \n",
    "    | StrOutputParser()\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Run the chain\n",
    "sql_query = sql_generation_chain.invoke({\"description\": description})\n",
    "print(sql_query)"
   ]
  },
